on $[0,3]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0.5$ |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292571 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x & y \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2(1)(1) & 3(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)(1)-1 \\\\ (1)^{3}-(1)^{2}(1)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.5 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}x & y \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}y & x \\\\ -2xy & 3{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}xy-1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}y+2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2(1)(1) & 3(1)^{2}-(1)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(1)(1)-1 \\\\ (1)^{3}-(1)^{2}(1)+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot (-2)}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.5 \\\\ 0.5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292573 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[1,\\,0]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[1,\\,0]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-3$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-2}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-3}} | $1$ |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292573 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3(0) & 2(0)-3(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 3(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3}-3(1)(0)+{0}^{2}-1 \\\\ {1}^{2}+{0}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}-3y & 2y-3x \\\\ 2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}-3xy+{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3(1)^{2}-3(0) & 2(0)-3(1) \\\\ 2(1) & 3(0)^{2}\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{1}^{3}-3(1)(0)+{0}^{2}-1 \\\\ {1}^{2}+{0}^{3}-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292574 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[-2,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $-2$ | $-6$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $-1$ |  |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,-2]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point  $[-2,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $-2$ | $-6$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | {{field-1}} | $].$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $-1$ |  |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292574 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2(-2)+2(1) & 2(-2)-2(1) \\\\ {1}^{2}-1 & 2(-2)(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+2(-2)(1)-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ (-2)(1)^{2}-(-2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-x+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}2x+2y & 2x-2y \\\\ {y}^{2}-1 & 2xy\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+2xy-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-x+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}2(-2)+2(1) & 2(-2)-2(1) \\\\ {1}^{2}-1 & 2(-2)(1)\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}(-2)^{2}+2(-2)(1)-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ (-2)(1)^{2}-(-2)+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292576 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [-1,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [-1,0]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-1]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292576 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(2)(-1)^{3}+1 & 3(2)^{2}(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\ 3(2)^{2} & -4(-1)^{3}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2 \\\\ (2)^{3}-(-1)^{4}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 14 \\\\ 12 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 4-14\\cdot 12}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -14 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{180}[\\begin{aligned}-82 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{139}{90} \\\\ -\\frac{53}{60}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.544 \\\\ -0.883\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3}+1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-2y \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & -4{y}^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-{y}^{2}+x \\\\ {x}^{3}-{y}^{4}-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(2)(-1)^{3}+1 & 3(2)^{2}(-1)^{2}-2(-1) \\\\ 3(2)^{2} & -4(-1)^{3}\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}-(-1)^{2}+2 \\\\ (2)^{3}-(-1)^{4}-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 14 \\\\ 12 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-3)\\cdot 4-14\\cdot 12}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -14 \\\\ -12 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{180}\\begin{bmatrix}-82 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{139}{90} \\\\ -\\frac{53}{60}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}1.544 \\\\ -0.883\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186292577 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [1,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292577 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+4)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(1) & 3(2)^{2} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}-3(2) & -3(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}+(2)^{3}-6 \\\\ (1)^{3}-3(1)(2)+4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 12 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-3)-12\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{30}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{10} \\\\ \\frac{53}{30}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.9 \\\\ 1.767\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-3xy+4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-3xy+4)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 3{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3y & -3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{3}-6 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3xy+4\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(1) & 3(2)^{2} \\\\ 3(1)^{2}-3(2) & -3(1)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2}+(2)^{3}-6 \\\\ (1)^{3}-3(1)(2)+4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 12 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-3)-12\\cdot (-3)}\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -12 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{30}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{10} \\\\ \\frac{53}{30}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}0.9 \\\\ 1.767\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186292579 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[2,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-12$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-8$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1.9$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,3]\\times [1,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting  at the point $[2,\\,2]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration.\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $-12$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-8$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}=[$ | $1.9$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292579 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(2)^{3} & -3(2)^{2} \\\\ 2(2)-2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{2}^{4}-{2}^{3} \\\\ {2}^{2}-(2)(2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}32 & -12 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.9 \\\\ 2.4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-xy+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{bmatrix}4{x}^{3} & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ 2x-y & -x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{4}-{y}^{3} \\\\ {x}^{2}-xy+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}4(2)^{3} & -3(2)^{2} \\\\ 2(2)-2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-\\begin{bmatrix}{2}^{4}-{2}^{3} \\\\ {2}^{2}-(2)(2)+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\begin{bmatrix}32 & -12 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-0.1 \\\\ 0.4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.9 \\\\ 2.4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292581 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.25,\\,0.25]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $1.095$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,-1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.25,\\,0.25]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $1.095$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292581 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+y-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1+3(0.25) & 3(0.25)^{2}+3(1.25) \\\\ 3(1.25)^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1.25+(0.25)^{3}+3(1.25)(0.25)-4 \\\\ (1.25)^{3}+0.25-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}1.75 & 3.9375 \\\\ 4.6875 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1.796875 \\\\ 0.203125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{1.75\\cdot 1-3.9375\\cdot 4.6875}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3.9375 \\\\ -4.6875 & 1.75\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1.796\\,875 \\\\ 0.203\\,125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{16.707\\,031…}[\\begin{aligned}-2.596\\,679… \\\\ 8.778\\,320…\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1.095 \\\\ 0.775\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+y-2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}1+3y & 3{y}^{2}+3x \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}x+{y}^{3}+3xy-4 \\\\ {x}^{3}+y-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}1+3(0.25) & 3(0.25)^{2}+3(1.25) \\\\ 3(1.25)^{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}1.25+(0.25)^{3}+3(1.25)(0.25)-4 \\\\ (1.25)^{3}+0.25-2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}1.75 & 3.9375 \\\\ 4.6875 & 1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1.796875 \\\\ 0.203125\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{1.75\\cdot 1-3.9375\\cdot 4.6875}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3.9375 \\\\ -4.6875 & 1.75\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1.796\\,875 \\\\ 0.203\\,125\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1.25 \\\\ 0.25\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{16.707\\,031…}\\begin{bmatrix}-2.596\\,679… \\\\ 8.778\\,320…\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}1.095 \\\\ 0.775\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186292583 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [0,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-3,\\,3]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $-2.621$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-3,0]\\times [0,3]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-3,\\,3]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}\\,≈[$ | $-2.621$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292583 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-3)(3)^{3}-1 & 3(-3)^{2}(3)^{2}+4(3)^{3} \\\\ 3(-3)^{2}-2(3) & -2(-3)+2(3)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-3)^{2}(3)^{3}-(-3)+{3}^{4}-3 \\\\ (-3)^{3}-2(-3)(3)+{3}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-163 & 351 \\\\ 21 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-163)\\cdot 12-351\\cdot 21}[\\begin{aligned}12 & -351 \\\\ -21 & -163\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}324 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{9327}[\\begin{aligned}3537 \\\\ -6967\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.621 \\\\ 2.253\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x{y}^{3}-1 & 3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4{y}^{3} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}-x+{y}^{4}-3 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2xy+{y}^{2}+1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(-3)(3)^{3}-1 & 3(-3)^{2}(3)^{2}+4(3)^{3} \\\\ 3(-3)^{2}-2(3) & -2(-3)+2(3)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(-3)^{2}(3)^{3}-(-3)+{3}^{4}-3 \\\\ (-3)^{3}-2(-3)(3)+{3}^{2}+1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-163 & 351 \\\\ 21 & 12\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}324 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-163)\\cdot 12-351\\cdot 21}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -351 \\\\ -21 & -163\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}324 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{9327}\\begin{bmatrix}3537 \\\\ -6967\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}-2.621 \\\\ 2.253\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186292585 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[2,5]\\times [0,5]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[5,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[5,\\,5]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2.727$ |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[2,5]\\times [0,5]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[5,\\,0]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[5,\\,5]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $2.727$ |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292585 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y-20) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y-20)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(5) & 2(5) \\\\ 2(5) & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(5)^{2}+(5)^{2}-25 \\\\ (5)^{2}-5-20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{10\\cdot (-1)-10\\cdot 10}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}25 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{110}[\\begin{aligned}-25 \\\\ -250\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{105}{22} \\\\ \\frac{30}{11}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}4.773 \\\\ 2.727\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y-20) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y-20)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2x & 2y \\\\ 2x & -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-25 \\\\ {x}^{2}-y-20\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}2(5) & 2(5) \\\\ 2(5) & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(5)^{2}+(5)^{2}-25 \\\\ (5)^{2}-5-20\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 \\\\ 10 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{10\\cdot (-1)-10\\cdot 10}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}25 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{110}\\begin{bmatrix}-25 \\\\ -250\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{105}{22} \\\\ \\frac{30}{11}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}4.773 \\\\ 2.727\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186292588 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,1]\\times [-8,8]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[0,\\,4]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-2,1]\\times [-8,8]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[0,\\,4]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292588 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-x-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3(0)^{2}(4)-1 & (0)^{3} \\\\ (4)^{2} & 2(0)(4)-2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}(4)-0-1 \\\\ (0)(4)^{2}-2(4)+2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 16}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 22\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}y-x-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}y-x-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-2y+2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}^{2}y-1 & {x}^{3} \\\\ {y}^{2} & 2xy-2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}^{3}y-x-1 \\\\ x{y}^{2}-2y+2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}3(0)^{2}(4)-1 & (0)^{3} \\\\ (4)^{2} & 2(0)(4)-2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}(0)^{3}(4)-0-1 \\\\ (0)(4)^{2}-2(4)+2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 16 & -2\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-2)-0\\cdot 16}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 22\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292589 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" "Suppose we wish to find a zero of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,3]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process that starts with ${x}_{0}=[3,\\,2]^{T}.$ Round your answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |"] [:db/cas 17592186292589 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(3) & -2(2) \\\\ 2(3) & 2(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(3)^{2}-(2)^{2}-4 \\\\ (3)^{2}+(2)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}12 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{12\\cdot 4-(-4)\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{72}[\\begin{aligned}60 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}2.167 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4x & -2y \\\\ 2x & 2y\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}2{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}4(3) & -2(2) \\\\ 2(3) & 2(2)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2(3)^{2}-(2)^{2}-4 \\\\ (3)^{2}+(2)^{2}-8\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}12 & -4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{12\\cdot 4-(-4)\\cdot 6}\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{72}\\begin{bmatrix}60 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{13}{6} \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & ≈\\begin{bmatrix}2.167 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186292590 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 10(1)-3 \\\\ 10(1)-3 & 10(2)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-10(2)+5(1)^{2}-3(1)-2 \\\\ 10(2)(1)-3(2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-10 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-10)\\cdot 20-7\\cdot 7}[\\begin{aligned}20 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -10\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{249}[\\begin{aligned}-498 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}-3xy-2x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(10xy-3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(10xy-3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 10y-3 \\\\ 10y-3 & 10x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{\\begin{bmatrix}-10x+5{y}^{2}-3y-2 \\\\ 10xy-3x\\end{bmatrix}}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 10(1)-3 \\\\ 10(1)-3 & 10(2)\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-10(2)+5(1)^{2}-3(1)-2 \\\\ 10(2)(1)-3(2)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-{\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 20\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{(-10)\\cdot 20-7\\cdot 7}\\begin{bmatrix}20 & -7 \\\\ -7 & -10\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-20 \\\\ 14\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\frac{1}{249}\\begin{bmatrix}-498 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186292591 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f