ansformation matrices:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nHence, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $RQP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =RQP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186295100 :question/worked-solution "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation, $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretching, and $R$ be the matrix that represents the reflection. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,Q=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,R=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,\\,\n$$\n\nHence, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $RQP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =RQP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation, $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretching, and $R$ be the matrix that represents the reflection. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,Q=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,R=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nHence, the combined transformation can be represent