ion "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $S:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${S}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${S}^{′}(14,-7).$" "To find the image of $S$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $S:$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}14 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${S}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${S}^{′}(14,-7).$"] [:db/cas 17592186295178 :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${Y}^{′}$, the image of $Y(-2,-3)$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${Y}^{′}($ \n\n$-2$\n\n,\n\n$11$\n\n$)$" "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat are the coordinates of the point ${Y}^{′}$, the image of $Y(-2,-3)$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${Y}^{′}($ \n\n$-2$\n\n,\n\n$11$\n\n$)$"] [:db/cas 17592186295178 :question/worked-solution "To find the image of $Y$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $Y:$\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${Y}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${Y}^{′}(-2,11).$" "To find the image of $Y$ under the action of $T$, we first create a column-vector $x$ containing the coordinates of $Y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $x$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $Tx:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Tx & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe result, $Tx,$ contains the coordinates of ${Y}^{′}.$ Therefore, ${Y}^{′}(-2,11).$"] [:db/cas 17592186295179 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-7.$" "Consider the line $y=x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-7.$"] [:db/cas 17592186295179 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $y=x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,3)$ and ${B}^{′}(5,4).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{4-3}{5-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-9 & =x-2 \\\\ x-3y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the line $y=x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,3)$ and ${B}^{′}(5,4).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{4-3}{5-2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-2) \\\\ 3y-9 & =x-2 \\\\ x-3y & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186295180 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=2x+1$ and linear 