0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(16,7).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{7-(-1)}{16-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{8}{14}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-2) \\\\ 7y+7 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-7y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the line $y=\\frac{1}{3}x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(3,2)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 16 \\\\ -1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(2,-1)$ and ${B}^{′}(16,7).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{7-(-1)}{16-2}(x-2) \\\\ y+1 & =\\frac{8}{14}(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =\\frac{4}{7}(x-2) \\\\ 7y+7 & =4x-8 \\\\ 4x-7y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186295186 :question/problem "Consider the line $y=2x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-5.$" "Consider the line $y=2x+1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=-5.$"] [:db/cas 17592186295186 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the line $y=2x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-1,1)$ and ${B}^{′}(0,5).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{5-1}{0+1}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 4x-y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$" "First, notice that the line $y=2x+1$ passes through the points $A(0,1)$ and $B(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $A$ and $B$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${A}^{′}(-1,1)$ and ${B}^{′}(0,5).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${A}^{′}$ and ${B}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =\\frac{5-1}{0+1}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =4(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =4x+4 \\\\ 4x-y & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186295199 :question/problem "Let $T$ be the linear transformation with matrix representation $T$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{RS}$ has endpoints at $R(0,2)$ and $S(-1,1).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints $\\overset{―}{{R}^{′}{S}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{RS}$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${R}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,$ and $\\,{S}^{′}($ \n{{field-3}}, \n{{field-4}}\n$)$" "Let $T$ be the linear transformation with matrix representation $T$ given by\n\n$$\nT=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nA line segment $\\overset{―}{RS}$ has endpoints at $R(0,2)$ and $S(-1,1).$ What are the coordinates of the corresponding endpoints $\\overset{―}{{R}^{′}{S}^{′}}$, the image of $\\overset{―}{RS}$ under the action of $T?$\n\n${R}^{′}($ \n{{field-1}}, \n{{field-2}}\n$)\\,$ and $\\,{S}^{′}($ \n{{field-3}}, \n{{field